increments.description:
One additive migration, numbered with the next free number after 47 at merge:
- med_orders;
- med_order_events, with no-update and no-delete triggers;
- medications.med_order_id and helix_patient_med_orders.med_order_id, each with a unique or plain index. The Helix column is also added to HELIX_SCHEMA, because Helix tables are created after the runner.
No FKs and no rebuilds.

config.py:
- the _feature_on default-off helper;
- MED_ORDER_STORE_ENABLED and MED_ORDER_LIFECYCLE_ENABLED;
- an import-time refusal of lifecycle without the store;
- a features() map published on /api/health.

medorder_service core:
- LEGAL_TRANSITIONS, administrable(), projection_status_for() and fhir_status_for();
- record_event_in_txn and transition_in_txn, with the version check and a clinical_access audit on the caller's connection.
Nothing calls the service yet.

adopt_legacy() creates one med_order per unlinked medications row and reproduces today's administrability exactly:
- active or prn becomes prescribed, or verified when verified_by is set;
- needs_verification is set for llm_extracted and agentic rows;
- discontinued becomes stopped, with a stated reason;
- projection_status is the old status.
It runs at boot only when the store flag is on, in chunks of 500 rows per transaction, and lists for a patient adopt their rows when read.
Helix rows without a link are NOT adopted.
parity_report() and server/scripts/medorder_parity.py list: unlinked live rows, Helix rows without an order, CPOE medication rows without an order, and projection mismatches.

Vigil add-medication (vigil_routes.py:907) writes through medorder_service.create_chart_order in ONE transaction:
- the same medications INSERT and idempotency handling as _insert_medication;
- the med_order (prescribed, prescriber is the actor as today, entered_by_role recorded);
- the event and the clinical_access audit.
In record mode the gate stays where it is.

emar_service.record_administration, for linked rows only:
- refuses unless administrable();
- writes the administered or not_given event in the dose's own transaction.
The check keys on the link, not the flag. Unlinked rows behave exactly as today, and so does PRN.

medorder_routes.py routes:
- list, get and events;
- create, sign and cancel;
- the to-sign queue;
- admin parity.
All are def handlers. They answer 404 when the store is off, and writes answer 404 unless lifecycle is on. Scoping uses require_patient_access, enforce_patient_scope and _in_scope_or_404 (404, never 403), with a scope-denied audit row and log_phi_read on reads.

Sign:
- runs the gate inside the service: screen outside the transaction, then re-check the fingerprint and version inside, answering 409 screen_stale on a mismatch;
- requires medication.prescribe;
- shows the same-ingredient duplicate as a notice only.

Permissions: MEDICATION_VERIFY goes to the pharmacist, and to admin until MO-R4; mirrored in src/lib/roles.ts.
The route emits a 'medorders:update' socket event after commit.

verify() and reject():
- run the gate with the dose, route and the pharmacist's role; a dose block still goes back to the prescriber;
- correct placeholders on AI orders only, reusing _is_placeholder;
- bind the exact formulary product;
- create the supply row through helix_service.insert_order(conn) at status 'verified', with a transition row.

The Verify Queue routes and Helix reviewing->verified or rejected on a linked row both call them. Record mode mirrors today's two verifications; lifecycle mode makes them one decision.
Helix dispensed and received on linked rows write order events, and administered events carry the received supply id (the custody link).
/queue/verification shows the allergy list.
The UI changes are in PharmacyVerifyQueue and HelixOrderReviewModal.

New hold, resume and stop routes plus the service steps:
- resume re-runs the gate;
- stop is terminal and needs a reason;
- a held order keeps its projection status for readers, while the eMAR refuses it through the link;
- a stopped order's projection becomes 'discontinued';
- the case room is told, the same way Helix rejections are posted today;
- MedRoundSheet gets an 'On hold' group with no Administer button.

Once lifecycle is on at every site and parity has been clean for 14 days:
- remove the direct medications and Helix INSERTs from routes and services;
- the routes stop calling enforce_medication_safety themselves;
- an AST guard test enforces both.
Test fixtures may keep inserting unlinked rows.
